<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      ONE permission, and it exists for one reason: Notion sync (CLAUDE.md §8). `api.notion.com`
      is the only host this app may ever contact. A second host — analytics, crash reporting, a
      CDN, an image loader pointed at the open web, a remote font — requires asking the user
      first. This app has no telemetry.

      v1 declares no POST_NOTIFICATIONS: reminders are deferred post-v1 (ADR-009).

      ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE is for AndroidConnectivityObserver (M4.3) and nothing else. It is a
      normal permission — granted at install, no runtime prompt — and it reads network state
      only; it grants no access to traffic or to any network the user has not already joined.
      The observer is ADVISORY: it decides when a sync is worth attempting. Nothing in the
      check-off path awaits it (CLAUDE.md §1).

      FOUR MORE PERMISSIONS ARRIVE FROM WORKMANAGER'S OWN MANIFEST (M4.10) and are not declared
      here, because they are not ours to declare — they are the library's. Verified in the
      MERGED manifest, the way ADR-040 established:

        WAKE_LOCK                 held while a job runs, so a sync survives the screen going off
        RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED    WorkManager restores its own queue after a reboot, which is
                                  exactly what Plan.md M4.10 asks for
        FOREGROUND_SERVICE        declared unconditionally by the library. THIS APP NEVER USES
                                  IT: it is for expedited and long-running work, and SyncWorker
                                  is neither (see SyncWorker's KDoc for why not). It is left in
                                  place rather than removed with tools:node="remove", because
                                  removing it would turn a future setExpedited() into a runtime
                                  SecurityException instead of a compile-time decision.
        ...DYNAMIC_RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED_PERMISSION
                                  a signature permission the library defines for its own
                                  broadcast receivers. Scoped to this app; grants nothing.

      All four are normal (install-time) permissions with no runtime prompt, and none of them
      widens what §8 actually guards: api.notion.com is still the only host this app contacts,
      and there is still no telemetry.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />

    <!--
      PREDICTIVE BACK IS OPTED INTO EXPLICITLY (M5.4).

      `android:enableOnBackInvokedCallback` is the documented opt-in from API 33. Newer platforms
      turn it on by default above some targetSdk, but the exact threshold is an API detail, and this
      project does not write those from memory (CLAUDE.md §3.2's rule, applied outside Notion).
      Stating it costs one attribute and removes the question.

      ⚠ It was NOT verifiable from the app: `ApplicationInfo.isOnBackInvokedCallbackEnabled` is not
      public API — checked with javap against the compileSdk 37 android.jar, where no such method
      exists. What IS asserted, in HabitsShellNavigationTest, is the mechanism a predictive gesture
      needs: the app claims back exactly when it has somewhere to go.
    -->
    <application
        android:name=".HabitsApplication"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:enableOnBackInvokedCallback="true"
        android:fullBackupContent="false"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Steady">

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Steady"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          ⚠ WORKMANAGER'S DEFAULT INITIALISER IS REMOVED ON PURPOSE. Do not "restore" it.

          HabitsApplication implements Configuration.Provider to supply a HiltWorkerFactory, and
          the official documentation is explicit that the default initialiser must be removed for
          a custom configuration to take effect (verified live, M4.10). Leave it in and the
          configuration is built and then ignored: WorkManager uses the default factory, which
          cannot construct a worker that has constructor dependencies. The failure is at RUNTIME,
          inside a background job, and it names the worker class rather than this file.

          Only WorkManager's entry is removed, not the whole provider — androidx.startup is shared,
          and node="merge" keeps it available for anything else that registers there.
        -->
        <provider
            android:name="androidx.startup.InitializationProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.androidx-startup"
            android:exported="false"
            tools:node="merge">
            <meta-data
                android:name="androidx.work.WorkManagerInitializer"
                android:value="androidx.startup"
                tools:node="remove" />
        </provider>

        <!--
          HOME SCREEN WIDGETS (M15.6b).

          ⚠ The receiver is NOT exported. A widget provider is driven by the system's own
          AppWidgetService, which holds BIND_APPWIDGET and reaches it regardless; exporting it would
          let any app on the device broadcast APPWIDGET_UPDATE at us for nothing in return.

          android:label is the row the user sees in the widget picker. The description and the
          footprint live in @xml/habit_grid_widget_info.

          ⚠⚠ android:icon IS STATED RATHER THAN INHERITED, AND M15.9 IS WHY. A receiver with no icon
          reports the APPLICATION's, which sounds identical and is not: the picker resolves that
          through the launcher's own preview cache, and on the user's phone that cache was still
          serving the pre-M14.1 mark months after the icon had been replaced — *"when I want to add a
          widget I notice that the icon is the old one"*. Naming the resource here is what makes the
          answer come from this build rather than from whatever the launcher remembers.

          ⭐ On API 35+ it is not what they will see at all: AppWidgetPreviewPublisher pushes a real
          composed preview of the widget, and that wins. This is the floor beneath it.

          ⚠ The configuration activity IS exported, and it has to be: the launcher starts it, from
          another process, when a widget is dropped. It is safe to export because it does nothing
          on its own - it reads EXTRA_APPWIDGET_ID and finishes immediately when that is absent,
          which is what a launch from anywhere else looks like.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".feature.widget.HabitGridWidgetReceiver"
            android:exported="false"
            android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
            android:label="@string/widget_habit_label">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.appwidget.action.APPWIDGET_UPDATE" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.appwidget.provider"
                android:resource="@xml/habit_grid_widget_info" />
        </receiver>

        <activity
            android:name=".feature.widget.HabitWidgetConfigActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Steady">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.appwidget.action.APPWIDGET_CONFIGURE" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          M15.11: repaints every widget at local midnight, so the habit widget's "today" square
          empties for the new day. Reached only through this app's own PendingIntent, from an
          inexact, non-waking alarm (see MidnightRepaint.kt), so it is NOT exported.

          ⚠ An explicit closing tag, not a self-closing one: EveryWidgetIsRefreshedTest and
          EveryWidgetHasAPreviewTest read each receiver up to the next closing tag, and a
          self-closing one would borrow the measure widget's body and be mistaken for a widget.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".feature.widget.MidnightRepaintReceiver"
            android:exported="false"></receiver>

        <receiver
            android:name=".feature.widget.MeasureWidgetReceiver"
            android:exported="false"
            android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
            android:label="@string/widget_measure_label">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.appwidget.action.APPWIDGET_UPDATE" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.appwidget.provider"
                android:resource="@xml/measure_widget_info" />
        </receiver>

        <activity
            android:name=".feature.widget.MeasureWidgetConfigActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Steady">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.appwidget.action.APPWIDGET_CONFIGURE" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

    </application>

</manifest>
